nancy@genie.slhs.udel.edu writes:
 
>Betsy asked about how we get from here to a free society as smoothly
>as possible--I've come up with a first step that sounds plausible
>to me.
>
>Get victimless crime laws eliminated--and then keep pressure on to
>the government to lower taxes rather than spend the money on something
>else. (Remember the peace dividend?)
 
(I'm sure you meant "Get laws prohibiting victimless crimes eliminated"?)
Minor quibbles aside (such as that where there is no victim, there can be no
crime), this would actually be the only step we would, as a society, need to
take. Unfortunately, I doubt it can be accomplished, even strictly as a
political problem. Far better for each of us to fight against such laws on an
individual basis, forcing the State's entities to fragment their attacks.
Governments will always tend to attempt increasing of power and confiscated
monies, it's just a fact of life. THe question that should be asked is, what
is the proper use of defense against such people?
 
>With a little luck, the combination should encourage people to
>think of their money as their own rather than something that
>the government lets them keep if there's nothing more important
>that needs to be done and promote an attitude of "is this law
>really necessary?".
 
Most people instinctively know their money is theirs, and resent having a
portion of it stolen even when they outwardly proclaim stereotypical
'liberal' beliefs. We know the difference between proper and improper
force because of the kind of animals we are.
 
>Any other ideas?
 
A lot of parent's lines, cliche as they sound, often turn out true. The one
that springs to mind at this moment is, "If everyone else jumped off the
Empire State Building, would you do it too?"
 
"The fault, dear Brutus, lies in ourselves." Most people get into a lot of
trouble because they do what they're told, they don't ask questions, they sign
their names, lines and financial futures away just because they don't know of
any other way. If you want to be free, then free yourself. If you want to
educate others about the concept, you're welcome to try. But nobody can free
anybody but themselves anyway, so why bother? Live your life the way you see
best, and defend it against all attacks.
 
Cryptoanarchy will help people to mind their own business -- a trait lacking
and sorely needed in society at large if we want to avoid mass random
violence.
 
Machiavelli: "It is dangerous to free people who prefer to be slaves." Most
people don't see or feel their self-imposed fetters, because they have never
known of any alternatives to those chosen by "most people". When we see the
chains, we can at least make an informed decision based on information, rather
than suffering under the false impression that no choice is available to us
and we are somehow being "forced" down a path in life, rather than choosing
for ourselves. We may still choose to be slaves -- but at least we know it is
a choice. (Some people find this more disturbing; myself, if I'm really a
brain in a vat, I want to know... ;)
 
If you choose to cast off self-imposed chains and take full responsibility
for yourself, you will find a great truth: "There is no security in freedom,
only boundless opportunity."
 
 
