Thanks Tim! This is exactly what I am stumbling over. I see much of the new
technology as capable of being used for many purposes. Cryptography, for
example, can be used by individuals to protect their privacy, but can also
be used by the State to control individual's access to information. 

My personal opinion is that our technology is advancing far faster than our
meme set. We've been struggling for 45+ years over how to handle the atomic
bomb, because it was built by a government that was not capable (as perhaps
no government is capable) of seeing beyond the immediate crisis. (I'm not
arguing pro-bomb or anti-bomb, just that the government has been pretty
clueless about handling it)

Without some evolution of ideas, I doubt that a lot of the futures
envisioned by people here will come to pass. The richest Prometheans willl
buy private islands or mansions and bribe the State to leave them alone.
The amusing toys, like virtual reality, will be built, but sold like
Nintendo games, as something to distract people. Anything that really
threatens the structure of the state will be blocked, derailed, or
suppressed. I can imagine a law that blocks, for example, any encrypted
transmissions over the Internet backbone. 


Betsy

PS personal statement: I don't like the Welfare state. I'm not convinced
about anarchy. I am still searching for ideas that I like and I will make
up my own mind, flames or no flames. If asking questions about what the
future could be like isn't Extropianly correct, what is? 
   Is it written in the Extropian principles that one must have a fixed set
of political ideas? What about those of us who are still evolving? 
  Flames aside, I've become much more libertarian since I started reading
this list. I've been very impressed by some of the arguments here. Tim's 
statements of polemics don't really move me one way or the other, as he
tends to wrap his beliefs in emotional statements and unsophisticated
stereotypes. 
   Treating all the poor as an identical lump is unsophisticated. If
nothing else, some are in the process of working hard and getting out of
 being poor, some have escaped from more repressive countries than ours
 and are educating themselves, some are hard-working but lack the
imagination to rise economically, some are disabled or otherwise 
have a hard time, and some are, of course, bums and drug addicts and scum.
   I understand Tim's point that he is not responsible for anyone else. 
I'm not arguing against that point. In his own terms, however, I think
there's some value in spreading some of the memes here. You are going to
need hard-working capitalist factory workers to build some of this
technology, in the decades or century before nano-manufacturing becomes
feasible. And, the more people you convert, the fewer will be shooting at
you later :-)
   PPS some of the hardest-line libertarians I've ever met have been poor
