Betsy Schwartz writes: <Do any Extropians have any desire , or believe that it
is possible, to see our current State evolve into a more free one? Has anyone
here drawn any sketches or ideas of how to actually make the transition from
today's Real to tomorrow's Ideal?>

The answer to question 1 is yes, democratic states evolve into more free ones if
the People want more freedom.  Extropians should be leading the charge to get
the People to want more freedom.  No revolutions, please, the '60s were
revolting enough.  All that's really needed is to steer the People away from
their current infatuation with group rights and head back towards individual
rights.  This split into groups has undermined the comity that used to be the
source of the Constitution's legitimacy.  We must reforge individual
second-person social bonds, (the "We" in We the People), or the decline of
western civilization will continue to proceed headlong.

The answer to question 2 is that the question is framed dangerously.  Please, no
more Utopias, they fail miserably.  Let's not posit an "Ideal" state at all.
Let's just posit processes to work towards, starting with Extropy.  Transitions
from today's Real can only end up at tomorrow's Real, there are no other
possibilities.  If we do not ground ourselves in how the People really are, and
do this continuously, then we are just another Utopian bunch of futurist
malcontents like every other Utopian bunch of futurist malcontents that have
failed to renew declining civilizations.  You want growth of
technologically-based free civilization to further our desires?  Then we must
act the sergeant and get the troops to follow our lead.

