Thus, "But what about the poor?" is met with a simple type of answer:
"What _about_ the poor?" Alternate forms of this answer are: "Do
nothing," "Go have a beer," and "Not my problem, thanks."

I can see some ways the Sufic or Zen view would relate. One shows that
the very question, "What about the poor?." is a misplaced question, a
disconnect. Equivalent to asking what we are going to do about the
color of the sky.

Come to think of it, even Christian teachings say "The poor shall
always be with us" and the most Extropian saying of all-time (well,
for Christians it is): "Give a man a fish and he eats for one day,
teach a man to fish and he eats for the rest of his life."

I want the inner city residents to escape their poverty by trading
with me, by having something of value I am willing to give them money
for. Some already do. 

But rather than having "transitional" strategies which continue to
extort more taxes from me (on pain of confiscation and imprisonment,
or worse), I want to see a complete end to "transfer payments" such as
welfare and AFDC (Aid to Fuckin' Drooling Cretins, as Simon! has
taught us).

Michigan has done this, as Harry Shapiro reminded me in a phone
conversation we had recently, and the result has been a dramatic drop
in folks seeking welfare. Maybe they've fled to other states with more
relaxed requirements.

I know that here in California the state is deeply in debt, is running
a deeper deficit this year that last, has a marginal tax rate of 12%,
a state sales tax of 8.25%, high property taxes (I just paid the first
half of my $2200 yearly bite), and all kinds of other special fees,
$300 tickets for driving in the wrong lane ($50 ticket, $250 for court
fees, special charges, etc.), and so on. And yet the state is sinking
into the Pacific under the weight of welfare cases, illegal
immmigrants using up social services, subsidies to semi-public
organizations, and layers upon layers of state bureaucrats.

It can't last much longer.

I suspect most of you agree. The link with the issues raised by others
here is whether we argue for mild, gentle, reasonable solutions or for
something stronger. Or whether we even argue at all, given that the
Unconverted are demonstrably unlikely to be converted to our system of
beliefs (as Elias Israel, Fred Moulton, and others have discussed
recently).

I think this list of some 300+ folks and perhaps 50 fairly active
participants (more than one post a week) is in fact a "safe haven" for
discussion somewhat extreme, unconventional, outre topics. As one
example, that I have not jumped in on, the topic of _eugenics_ just
came up, with someone arguing for deliberate impovement of the human
stock by selective breeding. This is considered "undiscussable" out
there in the nonExtropian world, and is results in comparisons to
Nazism even faster than my "Do nothing" comments did. Yet it seems to
be a perfectly fine Extropian topic.

So, I say we wear our extreme views proudly. We'll turn off many, but
we'll pull-in a few. And that's what matters the most. Not watering
down our odd views so that we can, with incredible effort that I think
few of us are prepared to commit to, someday reach a 2% popularity
rating.

(The LP took 20 years of effort, of speeches, of books, of funding, of
several bookshelves of books by Rand, Rothbard, Hospers, Hess, and
many others, and still it has barely penetrated the American
consciousness. I for one am not interested in starting this long, slow
climb to a minuscule impact. I will grant anyone that "libertarian
ideas" are in the air today, but they certainly would've been anyway
even without the LP.)

No, Extropians rightly believe technology is on their side, and that
certain things will happen with or without a mass movement.

Really, we don't need to lower our standards of discussion to pull in
more recruits.

--Tim May


