Date: Fri, 24 Dec 1993 12:42:55 -0800 (PST)
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: Jesuits, Memes, Cato, and Bestsellers

On the issue of "outreach" and whether Extropianism can ever be a mass
movement with broad popular appeal, and relating to Geoff Dale's
comments about a two-tiered approach, here are some ideas about what
we may be best compared to:

* Jesuits (Society of Jesus, Catholic Church). Not the religious
aspects--though I'm not sure how many Jesuits actually believe,
anyway--but the "elitist" aspects.

* The Jesuits don't water down their message to appeal to the masses.
They discuss radical ideas and thus act as a kind of think tank for
the Catholic Church.

(I suspect other religions, and of course secular organizations, also
have these kinds of think tanks. The Jesuits come to my mind first,
though.)

Extropians can have much more radical views than any mass movement is
likely to have. Views on uploading, cryonics, eugenics, anarchy, and
the like. Few, if any, of these ideas will appeal to the "man in the
street" and, as recent posts have shown us, to many of the people who
wander onto this List and then are shocked that "antidiscrimination
laws" are rejected out of hand by the vast majority of us.

Comparisons to the Cato Institute keep coming up, and it is tempting
to compare ourselves to them and hope (some do, not me) that we can
someday have a similar policy effect. This fits with the Jesuits idea,
too, though my skepticism tells me we're not like to find a group that
looks to us for thinking the way the Catholic Church looks to the
Jesuits (but then the Catholic-Jesuit relationship evolved over
several centuries...).

Frankly, I see none of us who can create such an organization as the
Cato Institute.

What I _can_ see is the glimmer of a possibility that a _book_ could
be written which makes our case, sort of a cross between Friedman's
"Machinery of Friedman" (or was it "Freedom"? Just a bad pun.) and
Harry Browne's "How I Found Freedom in a Unfree World," but with
liberal doses of the neo-tech, weird stuff that makes "Wired,"
"Mondo-2000," and "Boing-Boing" sell so well to certain types of
people.

If the book hit a responsive chord, and was well done, it could
conceivably become a best-seller. This would boost Extropian ideas
immensely. Pop culture, and all that.

Just a thought.


--Tim May

